course: euphemisms
lesson: 14
title: Soft words for hard things: relations and family and children's behaviour
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Parents and carers use soft words about children's bad behaviour so they do not sound cross or shame the child in public.
explain: «He's just tired» often means he is screaming, and «she's spirited» often means she will not do as she is told.
explain: Family words soften hard situations too: a police «domestic» is a fight at home, and a «weekend dad» only sees his children at weekends.
explain: Vivid example: in the supermarket a toddler lies on the floor kicking and yelling, and his mum smiles at the cashier: «Sorry, he's a bit overexcited.»
fact: "A domestic" means a violent quarrel at home (police and news jargon, UK).
fact: "Weekend dad" means a divorced father who sees his children only at weekends.
fact: "Co-parenting" means parents who are apart still raising their children together.
fact: "Empty nester" means a parent whose children have grown up and left home.
fact: "Tired" said about a child often means cranky (grumpy) or having a tantrum (a fit of screaming and crying).
fact: "Overexcited" said about a child often means out of control.
fact: "A handful" means hard to control.
fact: "Spirited" means wilful (always wanting her own way) and hard to manage.
fact: "Strong-willed" means defiant, refusing to obey.
fact: "High-spirited" means noisy and unruly (not keeping to the rules).
fact: "Cheeky monkey" means a naughty or rude child, said fondly (UK).
fact: "Little terror" means a very naughty child (jokey).
fact: "Meltdown" means a screaming tantrum.
fact: "Going through a phase" means behaving badly for a while.
fact: "The terrible twos" means the difficult stage of a toddler around age two.
fact: "Threenager" means a defiant three-year-old acting like a moody teenager (jokey).
fact: "Time out" means a short punishment of being sent to sit alone.
fact: "The naughty step" means a place where a child must sit as a punishment (UK).
fact: "Made a bad choice" is teacher jargon for misbehaved.
fact: "Use your words" means stop hitting, screaming or whining and say what you want.
exercise: The news reports police were called to "a domestic." What happened? | A violent quarrel at home | A burglary at a corner shop | A broken boiler | A noisy house party
exercise: Leo's dad is a "weekend dad." What does that mean? | His parents are apart and Leo sees Dad at weekends | Dad works in another town and only comes home at weekends | Dad plays football at weekends | Dad cooks only at weekends
exercise: Mia's parents are "co-parenting." What does that mean? | They live apart but raise her together | They both work as teachers at her school | They have two children each | They share one car
exercise: Gran says she is "an empty nester" now. What does she mean? | Her children have grown up and left home | Her bird box in the garden has no birds in it | Her fridge is empty | Her house was burgled
exercise: The toddler is screaming and Dad says, "He's just tired." What does Dad really mean? | The child is cranky and having a tantrum | The child is fast asleep in his cot upstairs | The child ran a long way | The child is ill
exercise: "She's a bit overexcited," says the mum as her girl runs wild. What does the mum mean? | The girl is out of control | The girl is very happy about a present | The girl has a fever | The girl is about to sleep
exercise: Nan says baby Sam is "a handful." What does she mean? | He is hard to control | He is small enough to hold in one hand | He grabs at things a lot | He eats handfuls of food
exercise: The nursery calls Ana "spirited." What do they mean? | She is wilful and hard to manage | She believes in ghosts and talks to them | She drinks juice a lot | She is always happy
exercise: "He's very strong-willed," says the teacher. What does she mean? | He refuses to do as he is told | He can lift very heavy things for his age | He has a written will | He never gets ill
exercise: The report calls the boys "high-spirited." What does it really mean? | They are noisy and unruly | They are always cheerful | They live on a hill | They are very tall
exercise: Gran calls Tom "a cheeky monkey" after he pinches a biscuit. What is she saying? | He is naughty but she is fond of him | He looks a bit like a monkey from the zoo | He is good at climbing | He loves bananas
exercise: The neighbour calls the twins "little terrors." What does she mean? | They are very naughty | They are scared of everything | They are very small for their age | They love watching scary films
exercise: "She had a meltdown in the shop." What happened? | She had a screaming tantrum | She got far too hot in the queue | Her ice cream melted | She fainted from the heat
exercise: Mum says her teenager is "going through a phase." What does she mean? | He is behaving badly for a while | He is studying the changing shape of the Moon | He is growing fast | He is changing schools
exercise: What are "the terrible twos"? | The difficult toddler stage around age two | Two very naughty twins who live next door | A bad mark of two | Two scary films
exercise: Dad calls his daughter "a threenager." What is she? | A defiant three-year-old acting like a teen | A girl aged thirteen | A child with three siblings | A child who is three times bigger than the others
exercise: The teacher says, "Time out, Jack." What must Jack do? | Sit away alone for a short while as a punishment | Go out to play in the yard for a while with friends | Stop the clock | Leave school early
exercise: In a British home, what is "the naughty step"? | A place where a child sits as a punishment | A broken stair that children must not use | A step children jump off | A dance move
exercise: The teacher says, "Ben made a bad choice today." What happened? | Ben misbehaved | Ben chose the wrong lunch | Ben picked the wrong book | Ben voted for the losing team
exercise: A toddler is whining and Mum says, "Use your words." What does she want? | Stop whining and say what you want | Learn the new spelling words for this week | Write a letter | Read a book aloud
test: Mum on the phone: "Sorry, she's tired, she's been screaming for an hour." What is going on? | The child is having a tantrum | The child is sleeping | The child is resting quietly | The child went jogging
test: Neighbour, rough: "Your lad's a bloody handful, he's kicked my fence again." What is the neighbour saying? | The boy is hard to control | The boy has big hands | The boy is helpful | The boy is strong
test: News: "Police attended a domestic in Mill Road last night." What did the police deal with? | A violent quarrel at home | A lost dog in the street | A broken shop window | A car crash
test: Classmate: "Dad's a weekend dad now, I see him Sat and Sun." What is true? | His parents live apart | Dad works only at weekends | Dad is away on a holiday | Dad lives with him all week
test: Teacher's note: "Freddie made a bad choice at lunch and pushed a friend." What really happened? | Freddie pushed someone and misbehaved | Freddie chose cold pasta | Freddie forgot his lunch | Freddie chose the wrong friend
test: Mum, exhausted: "He's not even 3 and he's a total threenager." What does she mean? | He is defiant and moody | He is very tall | He acts very grown-up and helpful | He is thirteen
test: Aunt: "Kids gone to uni, house is so quiet. Proper empty nester now." What has happened? | Her children have moved out | Her pets ran away from home | Her birds flew south | She sold her furniture
test: Grandma laughing: "Oh, he's high-spirited, that one!" as he jumps on the sofa. What is she saying? | He is noisy and unruly | He is very happy and calm | He is good at high jumps | He believes he saw a ghost
test: Babysitter text: "bit of a meltdown at bedtime but she's asleep now" What happened at bedtime? | She had a tantrum | The heating broke down | Her ice lolly melted | She fell asleep straight away
test: Dad, snapping: "Right, naughty step, now. Three minutes." What is happening? | The child is being punished | The child is going upstairs to bed | The step is broken | It is a game
test: Mum to cousin: "Don't worry, it's the terrible twos, it'll pass." What does she mean? | The toddler's bad behaviour is a normal stage | The twins are terrible | Two is a bad number | Something bad will happen twice more before it stops
test: Parenting leaflet: "Co-parenting works best when both parents agree on bedtimes." Who is the leaflet for? | Parents who live apart but raise children together | Parents of twins who share all the work of bedtime | Grandparents | Teachers
test: Uncle: "Strong-willed? He's a stubborn little mule!" What do both phrases mean? | The child is defiant | The child is very strong | The child is very clever | The child likes animals
exam: Identify the meaning most accurately conveyed when a carer states that a child is "going through a phase". | The child is exhibiting temporary undesirable behaviour | The child is studying lunar cycles | The child is undergoing rapid physical growth | The child is transferring to another institution
exam: Select the response consistent with the established usage of the term "time out" in respect of the discipline of young children. | A brief period of enforced solitude as a sanction | An interval of recreation out of doors | A cessation of timekeeping during a game | An early dismissal from school
exam: It is stated that a child has been described as "spirited". Ascertain the quality thereby most probably intended. | Wilfulness and difficulty of management | A belief in supernatural beings | Constant cheerfulness and calm | An unusual fondness for beverages
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the instruction "use your words" addressed to a young child? | The child is to cease crying or striking and state the request | The child is to enlarge his vocabulary by learning new words daily | The child is to compose a written letter | The child is to recite a poem aloud
